      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 72.002, 429.96, 539.969, 455.847 ],
      "font" : "CMBX12",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Theorem 1.9. The modular curve X0(N) has density degree min(δ(X0(N)/Q)) equal to 5 if and only if N = 109.",
      "kids" : [ {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 5,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 324.72, 540.042, 420.327 ],
        "font" : "CMR12",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "This main theorem is an important step in determining all curves X0(N) with inﬁnitely many quintic points. In fact, if a curve has inﬁnitely many quintic points, then it has to have density degree ≤ 5. And our result completes the classiﬁcation of all modular curves of density degree ≤ 5. So, in order to determine all curves X0(N) with inﬁnitely many quintic points, it suﬃces to determine for the explicit list X0(N) of density degree ≤ 4 whether they have inﬁnitely many quintic points. With some extra work, we are able to prove the following:"
      } ]
    }, {
